{"classified_as":[{"_label":"Collection Item","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404024","type":"Type"}],"id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/actor/1074","identified_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"preferred term","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670"}],"content":"Saks Fifth Avenue","type":"Name"},{"classified_as":[{"_label":"first_name","id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/terms/local/first_name"}],"content":"","type":"Name"},{"classified_as":[{"_label":"last_name","id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/terms/local/last_name"}],"content":"Saks Fifth Avenue","type":"Name"}],"produced_by":{"carried_out_by":[],"timespan":{"type":"TimeSpan"},"type":"Production"},"referred_to_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"biography statement","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435422"}],"content":"Saks Fifth Avenue (originally Saks \u0026 Company; colloquially Saks) is an American luxury department store chain headquartered in New York City and founded by Andrew Saks. The original store opened in the F Street shopping district of Washington, D.C. in 1867. Saks expanded into Manhattan with its Herald Square store in 1902 and flagship store on Fifth Avenue in 1924. The chain was acquired by Tennessee-based Proffitt's, Inc. (renamed Saks, Inc.) in 1998, and Saks, Inc. was acquired by the Canadian-based Hudson's Bay Company (HBC) in 2013.Subsidiary Saks Off 5th, originally a clearance store for Saks Fifth Avenue, is now a large off-price retailer in its own right managed independently from Saks Fifth Avenue under HBC. (Sourced from Wikipedia May 2023)","type":"LinguisticObject"}],"representation":[],"type":"Actor"}